KG42.register("rules-5f6e31dafbcb8d164f8e9a08",{"id":"50-residential_commercial_multi_energy::v141-c-R003","paper":"50-residential_commercial_multi_energy","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R003","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01-fair-p2p-multi-energy-trading","name":"Local clearing price","process_stage":"pricing","market_meaning":"The model assumes that the two prosumers agree on a unified fixed price, to be determined, for electricity and for heating separately. Scope: Electricity and heating trading between the modeled residential and commercial prosumers. Trading prices are optimized by the Nash-type formulation with guaranteed benefit allocation fairness between the two prosumers. Scope: Fair electricity and heating trading prices between the two modeled prosumers. Trading prices are discretized into pieces; the heating-price formulation selects exactly one discrete price piece. Scope: Discretized energy trading prices; the explicit single-piece selection constraint is shown for the heating-price formulation. The electricity trading price has a lower bound of 0 $/kWh and an upper bound of 0.185 $/kWh_e, equal to the highest commercial grid electricity price during the peak period. Scope: Case-study electricity trading price between the two prosumers. Condition: Preliminary price-bound setting in this study. The heating trading price has a lower bound of 0 $/kWh and a conservative upper bound equal to the heating supply unit price when demand is supplied solely by natural gas boilers. Scope: Case-study heating trading price between the two prosumers. Condition: Preliminary price-bound setting in this study. The electricity P2P trading price should not be lower than the applicable feed-in price. Scope: Electricity P2P trading price under the source's conditional pricing guidance. Condition: Onsite generation feed-in back to the grid is allowed.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function pricing.local under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["pricing.local"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Case-study electricity trading price between the two prosumers. | Case-study heating trading price between the two prosumers. | Discretized energy trading prices; the explicit single-piece selection constraint is shown for the heating-price formulation. | Electricity P2P trading price under the source's conditional pricing guidance. | Electricity and heating trading between the modeled residential and commercial prosumers. | Fair electricity and heating trading prices between the two modeled prosumers.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g02-e20","v141-b-g03-e08","v141-b-g04-e04","v141-b-g04-e05"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"x_heat_trading_price","symbol":"C^{h\\_ex}","meaning":"Unified heating trading price between the two prosumers, described by the source as a continuous variable to be determined.","kind":"operational","domain":"continuous real","unit":"$/kWh_h","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e10","v141-b-g04-e04"]},{"variable_id":"x_electricity_trading_price","symbol":"C^{e\\_ex}","meaning":"Unified electricity trading price between the two prosumers, described by the source as a continuous variable to be determined.","kind":"operational","domain":"continuous real","unit":"$/kWh_e","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e10","v141-b-g04-e04"]},{"variable_id":"x_heat_price_piece_selector","symbol":"X_k","meaning":"Binary variable that equals 1 when discrete heating-price piece k is selected and 0 otherwise.","kind":"operational","domain":"binary {0,1}","unit":null,"indices":["k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e05","v141-b-g03-e11"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"p_e_lower_bound","symbol":"C^{e\\_ex,lb}","meaning":"Preliminary lower bound for the electricity trading price; the source prints 0 $/kWh.","value":{"kind":"numeric","number_value":0.0,"text_value":null},"unit":"$/kWh_e","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-e01"]},{"parameter_id":"p_e_upper_bound","symbol":"C^{e\\_ex,ub}","meaning":"Preliminary upper bound for the electricity trading price, equal to 0.185 $/kWh_e, the highest commercial peak-period grid electricity price.","value":{"kind":"numeric","number_value":0.185,"text_value":null},"unit":"$/kWh_e","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-e01"]},{"parameter_id":"p_h_lower_bound","symbol":"C^{h\\_ex,lb}","meaning":"Preliminary lower bound for the heating trading price; the source prints 0 $/kWh.","value":{"kind":"numeric","number_value":0.0,"text_value":null},"unit":"$/kWh_h","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-e01"]},{"parameter_id":"p_h_upper_bound_boiler","symbol":"C^{h\\_ex,boiler}","meaning":"Conservative upper bound for the heating trading price, equal to the heating supply unit price when heating demand is fulfilled solely by natural gas boilers. The source does not supply a general numeric value.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"$/kWh_h","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-e01"]},{"parameter_id":"p_feed_in","symbol":"C^{feed\\_in}","meaning":"Applicable feed-in price when onsite generation feed-in back to the grid is allowed. The source cites China gas-based power generation at 0.070 $/kWh_e as a reference, not as a universal value.","value":{"kind":"text","number_value":null,"text_value":"applicable feed-in price; China gas-based reference 0.070 $/kWh_e"},"unit":"$/kWh_e","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e20","v141-b-g03-e03"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The model assumes that the two prosumers agree on a unified fixed price, to be determined, for electricity and for heating separately. Scope: Electricity and heating trading between the modeled residential and commercial prosumers.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e04"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Trading prices are optimized by the Nash-type formulation with guaranteed benefit allocation fairness between the two prosumers. Scope: Fair electricity and heating trading prices between the two modeled prosumers.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e08"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Trading prices are discretized into pieces; the heating-price formulation selects exactly one discrete price piece. Scope: Discretized energy trading prices; the explicit single-piece selection constraint is shown for the heating-price formulation.","expression":"\\sum_k X_k = 1,\\quad X_k \\in \\{0,1\\}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_heat_price_piece_selector"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g04-e05","v141-b-g03-e11"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The electricity trading price has a lower bound of 0 $/kWh and an upper bound of 0.185 $/kWh_e, equal to the highest commercial grid electricity price during the peak period. Scope: Case-study electricity trading price between the two prosumers. Condition: Preliminary price-bound setting in this study.","expression":"C^{e\\_ex,lb} \\le C^{e\\_ex} \\le C^{e\\_ex,ub}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_electricity_trading_price"],"parameter_refs":["p_e_lower_bound","p_e_upper_bound"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-e01"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The heating trading price has a lower bound of 0 $/kWh and a conservative upper bound equal to the heating supply unit price when demand is supplied solely by natural gas boilers. Scope: Case-study heating trading price between the two prosumers. Condition: Preliminary price-bound setting in this study.","expression":"C^{h\\_ex,lb} \\le C^{h\\_ex} \\le C^{h\\_ex,boiler}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_heat_trading_price"],"parameter_refs":["p_h_lower_bound","p_h_upper_bound_boiler"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-e01"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C006","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The electricity P2P trading price should not be lower than the applicable feed-in price. Scope: Electricity P2P trading price under the source's conditional pricing guidance. Condition: Onsite generation feed-in back to the grid is allowed.","expression":"C^{e\\_ex} \\ge C^{feed\\_in}\\quad \\text{(when onsite-generation feed-in is allowed)}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_electricity_trading_price"],"parameter_refs":["p_feed_in"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e20","v141-b-g03-e03"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-a1cda160c2-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.1. Pricing strategy","anchor_text":"5.1. Pricing strategy As mentioned in Section 3.3, the energy trading prices are dis- Fi cretized in","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"As mentioned in Section 3.3, the energy trading prices are discretized into pieces, the number of pieces would affect the computational complexity. Considering both electricity and heating trading prices should vary within corresponding upper and lower bounds, the more precise of setting bounds, the more computationally efficient of the model. In this study, preliminary attempts set the lower bounds of both electricity and heating trading prices to 0 $/kWh. The upper bound for electricity trading price is set to 0.185 $/kWh_e, which is equal to the highest grid electricity price for commercial prosumer during the peak period. If the electricity trading price is even higher than this value, two prosumers would choose to purchase electricity completely from the grid instead of doing energy trading between each other. Meanwhile, the upper bound of the heating trading price is set conservatively equal to the value when heating demand is solely fulfilled by using natural gas boilers. This is due to the optimization result that does not configure natural gas boilers for heating supply although it is available in the model (CHP and heat pump are configured as analyzed in the next sub-section), which means using gas boilers as the heating supplier representing a possible but not preferable solution. The corresponding heating supply unit price could be a conservative value for the upper bound of heating trading price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-a1cda160c2-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.1. Pricing strategy","anchor_text":"s- Fig. 8. Optimal cost for two prosumers (RAC – residential annualized cost, CAC a- – commercial an","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 9","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"(1) If onsite generation feed-in back to the grid is allowed, the electricity P2P trading price should not be lower than that feed-in price. Otherwise, all prosumers may select to feed-in all surplus power back to the grid instead of conduct P2P trading. In China, the average feed-in price for gas-based power generation is 0.070 $/kWh_e [52]. Taking this value as a reference, the electricity P2P trading price should fall somewhere within the light-red area as shown in Fig. 9. Two benefits can be expected by narrowing the search space (between lower and upper bounds) of the fair trading price, i.e., one is speeding up the model solving as fewer binary variables are required to model the piecewise segments of the price; the other is providing better reference particularly for the decision-makers who do not have a mathematical model to precisely determine the fair price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e08","location":{"location_id":"src-a1cda160c2-p11-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":11,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"6. Conclusions pr To determine a fair pricing strategy for multi-energy P2P trading en between diffe","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"To determine a fair pricing strategy for multi-energy P2P trading between different prosumers is a complicated problem. This study establishes an optimization model to evaluate the P2P multi-energy trading between residential and commercial prosumers considering integrated demand-side management (demand response and three categories of storage). Both electricity and heating can be traded, and the trading prices are optimized by the Nash-type formulation with guaranteed benefit allocation fairness between two prosumers. The whole complex problem is further linearized by implementing McCormick Envelopes relaxation and piecewise linearization, which significantly reduce the computational complexity. The proposed model could be a practical and efficient trading aiding tool to determine fair trading prices, as well as provide insights into optimal energy infrastructure designs."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-a1cda160c2-p13-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":13,"printed_page":null,"section":"A.1.1 Residential prosumer's objective","anchor_text":"hence the unit prices (Cim and Cex) vary on the hourly basis. Note that the different. RGC = ( RChim","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-13.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In this study, we assume two prosumers would agree on unified prices (fixed value and to be determined) for electricity and heating trading, respectively. Note that the energy trading prices (i.e., C^{h\\_ex} and C^{e\\_ex}) are variables, and the amount of energy exchange during the trading are variables (i.e., Q^{rtoc}, Q^{ctor}, E^{rtoc}, and E^{ctor}) as well."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-a1cda160c2-p14-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":14,"printed_page":null,"section":"A.1. Objective functions and linearization","anchor_text":"Zk = Xk · H ctor Zk Xk ·M1 Zk H ctor Zk H ctor (1 Xk )· M1 Zk 0 where Xk is a binary variable, the p","equation_id":"A9a-A9i","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-14.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\sum_{s,h,i} Q^{ctor\\_heat}_{s,h,i} = H^{ctor} (A9a); C^{h\\_ex} = \\sum_k \\bar{C}^{h\\_ex} \\cdot X_k (A9b); \\sum_k X_k = 1 (A9c); C^{h\\_ex} \\cdot H^{ctor} = \\sum_k \\bar{C}^{h\\_ex} \\cdot X_k \\cdot H^{ctor} (A9d); Z_k = X_k \\cdot H^{ctor} (A9e); Z_k \\le X_k \\cdot M_1 (A9f); Z_k \\le H^{ctor} (A9g); Z_k \\ge H^{ctor} - (1 - X_k) \\cdot M_1 (A9h); Z_k \\ge 0 (A9i); where X_k is a binary variable, the product of two continuous variables (i.e., H^{ctor} and C^{h\\_ex}) can be linearized as the product of a continuous variable (H^{ctor}) and a binary variable (X_k) as derived by Eqs. (A9a)–(A9d). Then the product of the continuous variable (H^{ctor}) and the binary variable (X_k) in Eq. (A9d) is further linearized by introducing a continuous variable Z_k and a big-enough parameter M_1 as derived by Eq. (A9e)–(A9i). Note that the number of discrete pieces (k) is user-defined, the larger the number of pieces, the higher of the linearization accuracy."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-a1cda160c2-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"5.1. Pricing strategy As mentioned in Section 3.3, the energy trading prices are dis- Fi cretized in","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"5.1. Pricing strategy\nAs mentioned in Section 3.3, the energy trading prices are discretized into pieces, the number of pieces would affect the computational complexity. Considering both electricity and heating trading prices should vary within corresponding upper and lower bounds, the more precise of setting bounds, the more computationally efficient of the model. In this study, preliminary attempts set the lower bounds of both electricity and heating trading prices to 0 $/kWh. The upper bound for electricity trading price is set to 0.185 $/kWhe, which is equal to the highest grid electricity price for commercial prosumer during the peak period. If the electricity trading price is even higher than this value, two prosumers would choose to purchase electricity completely from the grid instead of doing energy trading between each other. Meanwhile, the upper bound of the heating trading price is set conservatively equal to the value when heating demand is solely fulfilled by using natural gas boilers. This is due to the optimization result that does not configure natural gas boilers for heating supply although it is available in the model (CHP and heat pump are configured as analyzed in the next sub-section), which means using gas boilers as the heating supplier representing a possible but not preferable solution. The corresponding heating supply unit price could be a conservative value for the upper bound of heating trading price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e03","location":{"location_id":"src-a1cda160c2-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"g p g p g $/ e [ ] g as value as a reference, the electricity P2P trading price should fall he somew","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Based on the above analysis on the optimal and fair energy trading prices, several insights can be identified as follows,\n(1) If onsite generation feed-in back to the grid is allowed, the electricity P2P trading price should not be lower than that feed-in price. Otherwise, all prosumers may select to feed-in all surplus power back to the grid instead of conduct P2P trading. In China, the average feed-in price for gas-based power generation is 0.070 $/kWhe [52]. Taking this value as a reference, the electricity P2P trading price should fall somewhere within the light-red area as shown in Fig. 9. Two benefits can be expected by narrowing the search space (between lower and upper bounds) of the fair trading price, i.e., one is speeding up the model solving as fewer binary variables are required to model the piecewise segments of the price; the other is providing better reference particularly for the decision-makers who do not have a mathematical model to precisely determine the fair price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-a1cda160c2-p12-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":12,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"η g g gy ηctoc_heat Heating loss rate during energy tra ηctoc_cool Cooling loss rate during energy t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-12.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"ηctor_heat  Heating loss rate during energy trading\nηctoc_heat  Heating loss rate during energy transmission among commercial buildings\nηctoc_cool  Cooling loss rate during energy transmission among commercial buildings\nηCHP_NG  CHP efficiency\nηb_NG  Boiler efficiency\nRAC¯  Upper bound of RAC for calculating ΔRAC\nCAC¯  Upper bound of CAC for calculating ΔCAC\nContinuous variables\nGAIN  Profit gain via energy trading\nPAY  Cost paid via energy trading\nGC  Grid cost\nMC  Maintenance cost\nFC  Fuel cost\nCAPEX  Capital cost\nCAPt  Installed capacity of each technology\nC_h_ex  Unit price for heating exchange between two prosumers\nC_e_ex  Unit price for electricity exchange between two prosumers"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-a1cda160c2-p12-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":12,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Esec_cool, h , i Electricity consumption by electric Eshp, h , i Electricity consumption by heat pu ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-12.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"E_s^ctor,h,i  Electricity exchange from commercial to residential prosumer\nE_s^rtoc,h,i  Electricity exchange from residential to commercial prosumer\nE^ctor  Sum of electricity exchanged from commercial to residential prosumer over hour season and buildings\nE^rtoc  Sum of electricity exchanged from residential to commercial prosumer over hour season and buildings\nH^ctor  Sum of heating exchanged from commercial to residential prosumer over hour season and buildings\nH^rtoc  Sum of heating exchanged from residential to commercial prosumer over hour season and buildings\nQ_s^ctor_heat,h,i  Heating exchange from commercial to residential prosumer\nQ_s^rtoc_heat,h,i  Heating exchange from residential to commercial prosumer\nX_k  1 select that piece of discretized energy price"}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-e04","quote":"In this study, we assume two prosumers would agree on unified prices (fixed value and to be determined) for electricity and heating trading, respectively."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e08","quote":"Both electricity and heating can be traded, and the trading prices are optimized by the Nash-type formulation with guaranteed benefit allocation fairness between two prosumers."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","quote":"As mentioned in Section 3.3, the energy trading prices are discretized into pieces, the number of pieces would affect the computational complexity."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-e05","quote":"C^{h\\_ex} = \\sum_k \\bar{C}^{h\\_ex} \\cdot X_k (A9b); \\sum_k X_k = 1 (A9c)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-e05","quote":"where X_k is a binary variable"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","quote":"In this study, preliminary attempts set the lower bounds of both electricity and heating trading prices to 0 $/kWh. The upper bound for electricity trading price is set to 0.185 $/kWh_e, which is equal to the highest grid electricity price for commercial prosumer during the peak period."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","quote":"In this study, preliminary attempts set the lower bounds of both electricity and heating trading prices to 0 $/kWh."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","quote":"Meanwhile, the upper bound of the heating trading price is set conservatively equal to the value when heating demand is solely fulfilled by using natural gas boilers."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e20","quote":"If onsite generation feed-in back to the grid is allowed, the electricity P2P trading price should not be lower than that feed-in price."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"6d242786ac39fafe8999ef4d06cc65eac7054b2d1eb3eb39f76c6f0dfb0c2115","applicability":"formalized","reason":"The frozen rule contains source-supported local trading-price variables and explicit pricing expressions: the heating-price single-piece selector in Eq. (A9c), and the source-stated electricity and heating price bounds. C003-C006 are formalized with source-scoped variables and parameters; C001 and C002 remain procedural because they state the unified-price assumption and Nash fairness motivation rather than a self-contained unary pricing expression in the supplied evidence. No graph edges exist in the frozen rule, so bindings attach to pricing.local only. All B evidence remains unreviewed under this experiment.","bindings":[{"object_id":"x_heat_trading_price","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The heating trading price is the rule's local price decision. The frozen graph contains pricing.local as the only node for this pricing rule and no edges."},{"object_id":"x_electricity_trading_price","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The electricity trading price is the rule's local price decision. The frozen graph contains pricing.local as the only node for this pricing rule and no edges."},{"object_id":"x_heat_price_piece_selector","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"X_k is the binary selector for the discretized local heating trading price. It is attached to pricing.local; no edge is available in the frozen rule."},{"object_id":"p_e_lower_bound","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"This fixed lower bound constrains the local electricity trading price in pricing.local."},{"object_id":"p_e_upper_bound","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"This fixed upper bound constrains the local electricity trading price in pricing.local."},{"object_id":"p_h_lower_bound","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"This fixed lower bound constrains the local heating trading price in pricing.local."},{"object_id":"p_h_upper_bound_boiler","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"This source-described conservative upper bound constrains the local heating trading price in pricing.local."},{"object_id":"p_feed_in","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The conditional feed-in benchmark constrains the local electricity P2P trading price when feed-in is allowed."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The unified-price assumption is a procedural pricing fact attached to pricing.local; it is not formalized as an expression because it states a modeling assumption rather than a local equation."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The Nash fairness statement concerns how the local prices are optimized. It is attached to pricing.local and is not formalized because the complete Nash objective requires coordination variables and relations not present in this frozen pricing rule."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The single-piece selection equation for the discretized heating price is a local pricing constraint attached to pricing.local."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C004","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The electricity price-bound inequality constrains the local electricity trading price in pricing.local."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The heating price-bound inequality constrains the local heating trading price in pricing.local."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C006","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The conditional feed-in lower-price guidance constrains the local electricity P2P trading price in pricing.local."}],"revision_requests":["The source expression for the heating discretized price in Eq. (A9b) is transcribed with \\bar C^{h\\_ex} under a sum over k but without an explicit k subscript on the price level. C003 therefore formalizes only the explicit single-piece selection Eq. (A9c) and leaves the price-level indexing ambiguous.","C005's conservative heating upper bound has no source-defined numeric value or symbol; p_h_upper_bound_boiler is an explicit formal placeholder with unspecified value, preserving the source's statement rather than inferring a number.","C006's feed-in threshold is conditional guidance, and the 0.070 $/kWh_e value is explicitly a China gas-based reference. p_feed_in is therefore left nonnumeric rather than treated as a universal bound."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
